• All 84 links added by the diff resolve. The remaining work is to distinguish direct owner-answer conflicts from merely historical examples and to check the exact scope of the spelling round.1458: decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: (1) passing a `&mut` on is plain lending, each call's use ending when it returns, with no reborrow concept or syntax; (2) a call is refused when an exclusive argument overlaps any other argument (`h(r, r)`, `h(&mut x, &x)`, `h(&mut a, &mut a.f)` refused); as tightened by OD15 (a) the same day, two arguments that borrow the same root conflict whenever one is exclusive, so even `h(&mut a.f, &mut a.g)` is refused; (3) `&mut` refers to the place, a write through it is the write to that place at once, and copy-in/copy-out is not part of the language (only an optimization proven equivalent); (4) `defer` keeps OWN-12 and a borrow passed to a deferred call stays live until the call runs; (5) one exit sequence on every non-abort exit: the exit value, then the scope's deferred calls (last registered first, innermost scope first), then the scope's loans end, then the remaining locals are destroyed; an abort runs none of it **Canonical current root-based exclusivity rule: REF-11 and OD15(a); this historical overlap formulation is a reference to that rule.**
1467: decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: `as` only when lossless by value domain; every lossy conversion is a named function **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): `as` is removed; the value-domain rule moves to one named lossless conversion.**
1471: decided 2026-10-29 by the owner. (a) Whole-owner freezing: a live borrow of any part of a value freezes the whole owner, and loans are keyed by the root binding (REF-11 as in the current compiler); this tightens OD2's part 2: at a call, two arguments that borrow the same root conflict whenever one is exclusive, even disjoint fields (`h(&mut a.f, &mut a.g)` is refused, as in the current compiler); OD1's "keyed by the root variable" stands. (b) The strict option: the language guarantees `&mut` exclusivity, which the checker enforces (OD2, OD15a), and no compiler pass or optimization may assume or exploit it until the checker's guarantee is proven in Lean (R-0499 with the Core semantics R-0496); until then the operational loan rules are defined now, G19 included: a pointer or alias derived from a loan is valid only while that loan lives. Now REF-11@3 (partial, bug 319, which OD23 adds) and REF-14 (pending, R-0496) **Canonical current root-based exclusivity subject: REF-11 (including OD23’s re-lent case); OD2 and OD23 refer here rather than defining separate variants.**
1479: decided 2026-10-29 by the owner ("strict across the board"): every argument of a call, the receiver included, is overlap-checked against the root, including re-lent `&mut` bindings, so `r.add(r.step)` is refused; read the Copy field into a `let` first. Now REF-11@3 (partial, bug 319) **Canonical current rule: REF-11 and OD15(a); this is the historical clarification of its re-lent case.**
1488: decided 2026-10-29 by the owner ("strict across the board"): no receiver auto-borrow: a method with `&self` or `&mut self` on an owned place is written `(&s).m()` or `(&mut s).m()`; a receiver that is already a reference is unchanged. Now OWN-15@3 (pending, bug 328) **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): no receivers and no method calls, so `(&s).m()` is gone.**
1498: decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Keep `==`/`!=` on bool/char and `<` on char; refuse ordering on bool
1515: decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `*h` + `destroy(h)`, no `free`, no `take` **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): a named move-out replaces `*h`; `destroy(h)` stays, no `free`, no `take`.**
1547: decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `x as T < y` is less-than; a generic cast type needs parentheses `x as (Vec<T>)` **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): `as` is removed, so the question no longer arises.**
1558: decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No `Float32` or `Char` aliases. Completed 2026-11-02 by the owner: the aliases `Int`, `Uint`, `Float64` and `Bool` are removed too ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-aliases))
1567: decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Accept field, refuse a reference to it **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): no packed structs until a demonstrated packed foreign ABI reopens them.**
1579: decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `as` never names a newtype
1593: decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse non-ASCII **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): there is no `char` type or character literal.**
1603: decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Accept: C order, no padding, align 1, stated in FFI-8 **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): no `repr(C, packed)` until a demonstrated packed foreign ABI reopens it.**
1607: decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Allow multi-level module paths `a::b::f`


